<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Gela</h1><p class="st-meta">688 BCE – 282 BCE (approx.) · capital Gela</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Rhodian and Cretan colony on Sicily's south coast whose tyrants, Hippocrates and Gelon, built the first great Greek power on the island.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Colonists from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lindos">Lindos</a> on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, led by Antiphemus and Entimus, founded Gela around 689 BCE on a long ridge by the Gela river, inland of a sandy coast. The colony pushed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicels">Sicel</a> interior and in 580 BCE founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/acragas">Acragas</a> to the west.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Gela reached its peak under the tyrants Cleander and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates-of-gela">Hippocrates</a>, who around 495 BCE dominated much of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelon">Gelon</a>, who seized <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a> in 485 BCE and moved half of Gela's people there. Its farmland produced grain and horses, and its terracotta workshops and coins with a man-headed bull were renowned. The playwright <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a> died there in 456 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> sacked Gela in 405 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timoleon">Timoleon</a> resettled it in the 330s BCE, but in 282 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phintias-of-acragas">Phintias</a>, tyrant of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/acragas">Acragas</a>, destroyed it and moved its people to his new city at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licata">Licata</a>. The site was refounded by Frederick II in 1233 as Terranova, renamed Gela in 1927.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gela.jpg" alt="Gela" width="320" height="189"><figcaption>Image: AbelDionis · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gela_Panorama.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>688 BCE – 282 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gela</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hippocrates, Gelon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-485&amp;focus=gela" aria-label="Gela on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5037 842 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gela lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5357 1098L5383 1099"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5383 1099h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5357 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5357 1098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gela. <a href="/atlas?year=-485&amp;focus=gela">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
